WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.333
(inspiring piano music)

00:00:09.685 --> 00:00:13.993
- Hawai`i is one of the
remote places on the planet.

00:00:13.993 --> 00:00:17.650
And in the open ocean,
there are no landmarks.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:19.885
So Hawai`i was discovered by canoes,

00:00:19.885 --> 00:00:23.135
with no GPS, no motors, no electronics.

00:00:23.988 --> 00:00:27.653
Just pure nature, and that's
what we're doing today

00:00:27.653 --> 00:00:29.682
to celebrate that.

00:00:29.682 --> 00:00:32.432
(water crashing)

00:00:37.328 --> 00:00:39.481
So we're on Hokulea,

00:00:39.481 --> 00:00:43.481
she is a replica of a
Polynesian voyaging canoe.

00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:51.470
They were the mode of
transportation for discovering

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.623
the Hawaiian Islands.

00:00:53.623 --> 00:00:56.264
- There's no metal, there's
no screws, there's no nails,

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:58.620
there's no braces that
hold the canoe together.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.787
It's nothing but lashings and of rope.

00:01:02.685 --> 00:01:04.188
There's no motors on board,

00:01:04.188 --> 00:01:06.302
there's no navigation
equipment that we use

00:01:06.302 --> 00:01:08.219
to go across the ocean.

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:14.540
We started in Hawai`i in end of May, 2014.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.744
We embarked on this worldwide voyage

00:01:16.744 --> 00:01:19.307
which first ended up going towards Tahiti,

00:01:19.307 --> 00:01:20.733
through our ancestral routes,

00:01:20.733 --> 00:01:24.268
and now we're here in Martha's Vineyard.

00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:27.115
(ship bell ringing)

00:01:27.115 --> 00:01:28.372
- A big part of this voyage, right,

00:01:28.372 --> 00:01:32.191
Malama Honua, is to train a whole cadre

00:01:32.191 --> 00:01:35.605
of young navigators and captains.

00:01:35.605 --> 00:01:36.906
Well wait, now we leave the back sail,

00:01:36.906 --> 00:01:38.656
let's get the 23 out.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:41.227
The big foresail.

00:01:41.227 --> 00:01:43.503
(intense music)

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:46.794
Kaleo Wong is one of the
apprentice navigators.

00:01:46.794 --> 00:01:49.111
I've done four voyages with him,

00:01:49.111 --> 00:01:52.060
and he was on his own
across the Atlantic Ocean,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.920
and he was brilliant.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.699
I mean, he was just so successful,
you know, after traveling like 1,200 miles,

00:01:58.699 --> 00:02:01.584
finding just little islands
in the middle of nowhere.

00:02:01.584 --> 00:02:04.591
You're talking like thousands of hours

00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:06.424
of observation at sea.

00:02:09.103 --> 00:02:11.215
- When we are navigating the open ocean,

00:02:11.215 --> 00:02:13.827
our biggest clue that
tells us where we are

00:02:13.827 --> 00:02:15.728
and where we're going is the sun.

00:02:15.728 --> 00:02:18.004
The sun, as we know, rises in the east,

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:19.101
and sets in the west,

00:02:19.101 --> 00:02:22.055
so if we just know where one point of is,

00:02:22.055 --> 00:02:24.899
then we know where everything else is.

00:02:24.899 --> 00:02:26.770
The stars do us the same thing,

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:28.331
just like the compass in the sky.

00:02:28.331 --> 00:02:31.989
We memorize close to 200
stars and know where they rise

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:35.641
and where they set and how
they move across the skies.

00:02:35.641 --> 00:02:38.726
- The sun and the stars and
everything work in conjunction

00:02:38.726 --> 00:02:42.055
with the swells, so in
the absence of the sun,

00:02:42.055 --> 00:02:44.577
then you maintain the
orientation of the crew

00:02:44.577 --> 00:02:45.956
into the swell patterns.

00:02:45.956 --> 00:02:48.456
That's a very difficult thing.

00:02:49.778 --> 00:02:52.703
The Hokulea was built
with the express purpose

00:02:52.703 --> 00:02:56.643
of proving that navigation
by the ancient way

00:02:56.643 --> 00:02:59.284
was very viable, that these
canoes could be guided

00:02:59.284 --> 00:03:02.291
over 2,000 miles and long distances.

00:03:02.291 --> 00:03:06.924
They proved that on their
very first voyage in 1976.

00:03:06.924 --> 00:03:09.727
What it means is continuation.

00:03:09.727 --> 00:03:13.181
Kaleo and these others, they're
just like a pinch of salt

00:03:13.181 --> 00:03:15.172
as far as the people who know how to do

00:03:15.172 --> 00:03:17.255
this type of navigation.

